    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making \"cold.\" A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it passes through a compressor that squeezes it, making it hot. Then it travels through coils on the back, where that heat escapes into your kitchen. Next, the refrigerant expands inside the fridge, which makes it very cold. Because cold things absorb heat, the refrigerant soaks up warmth from your food. It keeps cycling around, grabbing heat inside and dumping it outside. That's why the back of a fridge feels warm — it's releasing the heat it stole from your snacks!"
  }
